#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125513} :activity/id #uuid "665ce843-c897-5cef-9141-25ecb7cdc260" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050456 :topic/id #uuid "4a81b34b-9d6c-50ec-87c8-f6c706602e63" :topic/math-academy-id 1894} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125513 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125828 :tutorial/content "Consider the quadric surface\n\n$$z^2 = x^2 + y^2$$\n\nwhose graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/d5/d5a23dfa8bf6a0efec59763b9a2ec6d22d878f75babdbed9fc04dc8a7106f6ba.png)\n\nThis is an example of an **elliptic cone**. It has its **vertex** at the origin, and the $z$-axis is the **axis of symmetry** of the cone.\n\nMore generally, the equation of an elliptic cone whose vertex is at $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ is given by\n\n$$(z-z_0)^2 = \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2},$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants." :tutorial/id #uuid "bf9fab37-b71c-5d84-95d5-791cd41706a7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4924 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "8afa0f97-79d7-5556-8054-cf7a927fbc25" :step/math-academy-id 30715 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125514}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125514 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124585 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124396 :question/id #uuid "c351fdde-ce68-5fa1-97e1-7678211a3963" :question/math-academy-id "e-8216"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e0b4012-403b-5739-8f33-3c79111129fd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049477} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049699} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051081}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357748} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360456} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360457} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360458} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360474} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399275}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of Elliptic Cones"} :step/id #uuid "b83d2704-1cd8-5d3e-a5d6-3347b6842a7c" :step/math-academy-id 30716 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125640}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125515 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124586 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124397 :question/id #uuid "89ae8962-7ad4-5860-b070-8df4d92128ed" :question/math-academy-id "e-8217"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb7148a2-58cc-5023-845c-435b47e31c83" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124893} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284747} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357751} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360601} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382601}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Traces of Elliptic Cones"} :step/id #uuid "60b53775-0c8d-50d6-8793-5131aef5e1ab" :step/math-academy-id 30717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125516 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125829 :tutorial/content "So far, we've encountered cones whose axes of symmetry are parallel to the $z$-axis. \nLet's now consider the cases when a cone is oriented along the $x$- or the $y$-axes.\n\nFor an elliptic cone centered at the origin, the other two cases are as follows:\n\n- When the $y^2$ term is isolated on one side, the axis of symmetry is the $y$-axis.\n\n$$\ny^2 = \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}\n$$\n\n![](images/06/06c02be2e3ee96862abacc19885e34a8b3cb096a1b85417169022394839e7888.png)\n\n- When the $x^2$ term is isolated on one side, the axis of symmetry is the $x$-axis.\n\n$$\nx^2 = \\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} + \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}\n$$\n\n![](images/0e/0ef83c7e023ddcf5e408c7b8cb4e97deb7ece2f366628e019aa04ef90810c6f9.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "3ba615cd-207c-53f0-ac45-57c3b3f46b84" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4925 :tutorial/title "Alternative Orientations of the Axis of Symmetry"} :step/id #uuid "63150029-88d0-527b-9d21-8af46b91197c" :step/math-academy-id 30720 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125515}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125640 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125882 :tutorial/content "The traces of an elliptic cone are either intersecting lines, ellipses, hyperbolas, or points.\n\nFor example, let's find the traces with the coordinate planes for the elliptic cone given by\n\n$$\nz^2 = x^2 + y^2\n$$\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xy$-plane (when $z=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\nx^2 + y^2 = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to the origin $(0,0,0).$\n\n![](images/d5/d5a23dfa8bf6a0efec59763b9a2ec6d22d878f75babdbed9fc04dc8a7106f6ba.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xz$-plane (when $y=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{0}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ x & =\\pm z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to two lines in the $xz$-plane.\n\n![](images/ba/ba552154b10ae7919496031613005c60461272923c6cae262a757b6df89c398d.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $yz$-plane (when $x=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ y & =\\pm z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to two lines in the $yz$-plane.\n\n![](images/2e/2e22ee99e93475eabae9ce8caacee814d0066dd30042367668542485cdacd8a3.png)\n\nThere are a few other cases we should note:\n\n- The trace of our surface with the plane $z=k$ for $k \\neq 0$ is an ellipse:\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2 = k^2\n$$\n\n![](images/d3/d32e0c05b0bd5a55ff38183b362eebd44bf668dc9ee68ec5b6ae2274fdae934e.png)\n\n- The traces of our surface with the plane $x=k$ or $y=k$ for $k \\neq 0$ are hyperbolas:\n\n$$\nz^2-y^2 = k^2 \\qquad \\textrm{or}\\qquad z^2-x^2 = k^2\n$$\n\n![](images/23/23dd8112391406afd1da714df32d3a01ddb99b68c3b0885078ad76ebba23f9b5.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "efd10d7e-d93a-5f24-b896-67f70dc4aa16" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7841 :tutorial/title "Traces of Elliptic Cones"} :step/id #uuid "b8cf188e-213a-5603-a9b9-fc97304c8d5c" :step/math-academy-id 45285 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125516}}] :activity/title "Elliptic Cones" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124697}]}
